DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The bbaacckklliigghhtt utility can be used to configure brightness levels for reg‐
     istered backlights.

     Called without any arguments it will print the current brightness level
     of the first registered backlight.

     The options are as follows:

     --ff _d_e_v_i_c_e  Device to operate on.  If not specified,
                _/_d_e_v_/_b_a_c_k_l_i_g_h_t_/_b_a_c_k_l_i_g_h_t_0 is used.  If an unqualified name is
                provided, _/_d_e_v_/_b_a_c_k_l_i_g_h_t_/ is automatically prepended.

     --ii         Query information about the backlight (name, type).

     --qq         When querying the brightness level only print the value.

     _v_a_l_u_e      Set the brightness level to this value, must be between 0 and
                100.  A trailing “%” is valid.

     iinnccrr|++ [_v_a_l_u_e]
                Decrement the backlight level.  If no value is specified a de‐
                fault of 10 percent is used.

     ddeeccrr|-- [_v_a_l_u_e]
                Increment the backlight level.  If no value is specified a de‐
                fault of 10 percent is used.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Show the current brightness level:

           $ backlight -f /dev/backlight/backlight0
           brightness: 98

